  "notes": [
    "RS0 to RS3 select one of sixteen registers, so the VIA occupies sixteen addresses. Wire them to CPU A0 to A3 and decode the rest onto CS1 and CS2.",
    "Both chip selects must be asserted at once, and both should be qualified with PHI2 or the VIA will see bus noise as accesses.",
    "Timer 1 can free-run and toggle PB7, which gives you a hardware square wave with no CPU involvement. That is the usual source of a system tick or a tone.",
    "Timer 2 counts pulses on PB6 as well as time, so it doubles as an event counter.",
    "On the original NMOS 6522, reading Port A returns the pin states rather than the output register, so an output loaded down reads back wrong. Port B reads the register. The CMOS W65C22 fixes this.",
    "IRQ is open drain. Several VIAs and ACIAs share one interrupt line through a single pull-up, and the handler reads each chip's flag register to find out who shouted.",
    "The shift register has documented silicon bugs on the NMOS part and is best avoided; bit-bang through the ports instead, or use a W65C22.",
    "Ben Eater's 6502 build uses this chip, which is why it is back in production and easy to buy."
  ],
